From 77fab7a249ed71c3527519c7b2ad04c4acd4bd7a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 18:25:50 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] media: i2c: ov5693: Reprogram the sensor mode when starting a
 stream

The mode registers (crop, output size, binning) are only written by
ov5693_sensor_init(), which runs from the runtime PM resume callback.
With the 1s autosuspend delay the sensor often stays powered between
two streams, so a format set in that window never reaches the sensor:
the receiver then expects a different frame size than the one the
sensor sends.

This is what happens with PipeWire/libcamera, which typically probe a
format, stop and restart with another one. The result is one or two
frames followed by a black picture (or CSI-2 frame errors on IPU6).

Write the mode registers again right before enabling the stream.

Link: https://github.com/linux-surface/linux-surface/wiki/Camera-Support
Patchset: cameras
---
 drivers/media/i2c/ov5693.c | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/media/i2c/ov5693.c b/drivers/media/i2c/ov5693.c
index 02236f3db..7aa4861f6 100644
--- a/drivers/media/i2c/ov5693.c
+++ b/drivers/media/i2c/ov5693.c
@@ -976,6 +976,18 @@ static int ov5693_s_stream(struct v4l2_subdev *sd, int enable)
 			return ret;
 
 		mutex_lock(&ov5693->lock);
+		/*
+		 * The mode registers are otherwise only written when the
+		 * sensor is powered up. With runtime PM autosuspend the sensor
+		 * may still be powered from a previous stream, in which case a
+		 * format change made since then would never reach the sensor.
+		 */
+		ret = ov5693_mode_configure(ov5693);
+		if (ret) {
+			mutex_unlock(&ov5693->lock);
+			goto err_power_down;
+		}
+
 		ret = __v4l2_ctrl_handler_setup(&ov5693->ctrls.handler);
 		if (ret) {
 			mutex_unlock(&ov5693->lock);
-- 
